A small update for anyone checking my LinkedIn profile after receiving one of my applications. 🖐️

People say one of the first things recruiters and hiring managers do is check a candidate’s LinkedIn profile. So, in case you are wondering about the increasingly mysterious 2026 gap in my CV, here is my 2026, explained. 🙂

My last role was a parental-leave cover. The person I was covering returned, and my contract ended on 31 January 2026.
Since then, I have:
- searched, analysed, mapped and tailored my way through 200+ job applications;
- researched numerous companies, industries and roles;
- prepared for and attended more interviews than I can count 😄 If this was a competition, I could probably become a champion. If interviews were paid, maybe even a millionaire one day. 😂
- completed 10+ recruitment tests and 10+ cases/assignments;
- reached final stages several times and waited for the offer that never arrived;
- received plenty of “thank you for your time, effort and great case” messages;
- been considered a strong or top candidate more than once; 
- analysed the processes, what worked and what did not, and adjusted my strategy along the way.

And somehow, I am still feeling good — and congratulations, I am still available. 😄

But things have changed after all this time and experience. Just as your company is searching for a special candidate, I am now searching for something special too.
I am no longer looking simply for a job. I want a role where I can contribute, be valued, use my experience properly and work in a great environment — and preferably stay for a few years, because job searching is definitely not one of my favourite annual traditions. 😄

I have also worked on managing my mental “budget” and my time better, while continuing to learn and develop professionally using some very useful tools available today. One area I have become particularly interested in — and have had plenty of time to test in practice — is AI + human collaboration. While some people mainly discuss AI as a replacement threat, I find it much more interesting as an assistant and helping hand under my own supervision. It helps me analyse faster, organise better, learn quicker, it can take over repetitive tasks and sometimes simply make the day lighter. And yes, I still keep an eye on those lightning-fast transactions and check what the super-powerful machine actually did 😄🤖.

So, the mysterious 2026 CV gap, in short:
Searching. Researching. Learning. Interviewing. Solving cases. Taking tests. Reading. Analysing. Adjusting. Developing.

My next plan: put everything I have learned, experienced and practised to work in the right workplace. 

Very curious when that day comes. 🙂
